CASE 1301
PRINTFORML ───────────────────────────────
FONTBOLD
PRINTFORML  Physical Injury
FONTREGULAR
PRINTFORML ───────────────────────────────
PRINTFORML Physical injury, especially from combat, can severely interfere with daily life, often making it impossible to do daily tasks:
PRINTFORML Leg injuries make movement from one place take twice as long.
PRINTFORML Arm injuries make daily tasks harder to do.
PRINTFORML Injuries to the mouth, neck, or throat can make it impossible to communicate with others.
PRINTFORML Crippled stomachs and bladders induce incontinence, which is also not helped with the increased need to eat and drink.
PRINTFORML Damage to sexual organs reduce efficiency with them and can cause pain if not treated.
PRINTFORML
PRINTFORML It is often recommended that combat is to be best avoided to prevent such physical trauma, but there other situations where trauma can still occur.
PRINTFORML Tactical field care is considered to be the backbone of Tactical Combat Casualty Care and consists of care rendered by first responders or prehospital medical personnel while still in the tactical environment.
PRINTFORML The acronyms MARCH can help personnel remember crucial treatment steps while under duress.
PRINTFORML
PRINTFORML  MARCH
FONTREGULAR
PRINTFORML ───────────────────────────────
PRINTFORML %BREAKENG("Massive hemorrhage: The most potentially survivable cause of death is hemorrhage from extremity bleeds. It is recommended to apply a tourniquet for any life-threatening extremity hemorrhages. Tourniquets during tactical field care should be placed under clothing 2 to 3 inches above the wound, with application time written on the tourniquet.")%
PRINTFORML %BREAKENG("Airway: Non-patent or closed airway is another survivable cause of death. Airway injuries typically occur due to inhalation burns or maxillofacial trauma.  If a person is conscious and speaking they have a patent open airway, while nasopharyngeal airway could benefit those who are unconscious and breathing.")%
PRINTFORML %BREAKENG("Respirations: Tension pneumothorax develops when air trapped in the chest cavity displaces functional lung tissue and puts pressure on the heart causing cardiac arrest. Thus, open chest wounds must be sealed using a vented chest seal. Tension pneumothorax should be decompressed using a needle chest decompression (NCD) with a 14 gauge, 3.25 inch needle with a catheter. Ventilation and/or oxygenation should be supported as required.")%
PRINTFORML %BREAKENG("Circulation: It is more important to stem the flow of bleeding than to infuse fluids, and only casualties in shock or those who need intravenous (IV) medications should have IV access. Signs of shock include unconsciousness or altered mental status, and/or abnormal radial pulse. IV should be applied using an 18 gauge catheter and saline lock in tactical field care, secured by transparent would-dressing film.")%
PRINTFORML %BREAKENG("Head injury/hypothermia: Secondary brain injury is worsened by hypotension (systolic blood pressure under 90 mmHg), hypoxia (peripheral capillary oxygen saturation under 90%), and hypothermia (whole body temperature below 95 Fahrenheit or 35 Celsius). Medical personnel can use the Military Acute Concussion Evaluation (MACE), while non-medical personnel can use the alert, verbal, pain, unresponsive (AVPU) scale to identify traumatic brain injury. The \"lethal triad\" is a combination of hypothermia, acidosis, and coagulopathy in trauma patients. Since hypothermia can occur regardless of ambient temperature due to blood loss, the Hypothermia Prevention and Management Kit (HPMK) is recommended for all casualties.")%
CASE 1303
PRINTFORML ───────────────────────────────
FONTBOLD
PRINTFORML  Death
FONTREGULAR
PRINTFORML ───────────────────────────────
PRINTFORML Death is certainly fatal if not treated, however it does not mean the end for anyone if you can get them up before they decay.
PRINTFORML There is also the option of using magic such as Recarm or services such as the Resurrect-No-Nomitron, however these again require you to know these respective spells or pay a high fee.
PRINTL
PRINTFORML However, with just a defibrillator you can resurrect anyone without the use of magic.
PRINTFORML 1. Replace any terminally damaged or destroyed vital body parts using surgery. These include the head and its brain, and the torso including the heart, liver, and a lung. Note that they only need one lung.
PRINTFORML 2. Tend all wounds to prevent further hemorrhage.
PRINTFORML 3. Using a blood bag, inject blood into the patient. Lift the bag up to let the blood flow.
PRINTFORML 4. Pull out the defibrillator and turn it on. Once you hear it charging up, place the pads on the target's chest. If done properly they will get back up like nothing happened.
